# Panel 177's brief, `helper_consume_then_use`: the life ends inside a helper
# that takes the cell, `finish(@b)`, and the caller reuses `b`. The checker's
# half (item 3) decides inside one function and does not look into `finish`,
# so this is the shape that belongs to the runtime by design: the poison is
# written into the helper's copy of the cell when `obj_delete` ends the life,
# `@`'s copy-out carries it back to the caller's `b`, and the crossing into
# `obj_add` refuses it before C runs.

extern "dead-handle-through-a-helper-the-checker-cannot-see-into.h"
    record Json tag Obj
    function obj_new() -> Json acquires obj_delete
    function obj_delete(item: Json consumes)
    function obj_add(object: Json, string: cstr lent, item: Json consumes) -> i64

function finish(@j: Json)
    obj_delete(item: j)

function main()
    a = obj_new()
    b: Json @= obj_new()
    finish(@b)
    _ = obj_add(object: a, string: "b".cstr(), item: b)
    obj_delete(item: a)
